Title: The Count
Author: Fixture

INT. FIELD STATION - DAY

Snow ticks against the window. MARA scrapes frost from the glass with a thumbnail.

MARA
The relay is dead. Nobody answers on the long wave.

EXT. RIDGE - CONTINUOUS

DELL shoulders the pack. The ridge runs east into weather.

DELL
We have four hours of light and eleven miles of it.

INT. FIELD STATION - LATER

Mara writes the numbers on the back of a ration card.

MARA
I logged the last transmission. It was a count, not a voice.

EXT. TREELINE - DUSK

Boot prints fill with blue shadow.

DELL
Something walked this before us and it was not careful.

INT. RELAY HUT - NIGHT

A lantern swings. The cut end of the cable is bright as a coin.

MARA
The tower is standing. The cable is cut clean.

EXT. RELAY HUT - NIGHT

Dell turns a slow circle with the lantern held high.

DELL
Clean means tools. Tools mean people.

INT. FIELD STATION - DAWN

Mara holds the headset. Static, then a rhythm.

MARA
I spliced it. Give me the count again.

EXT. RIDGE - DAY

Cloud closes over the ridge like a lid.

DELL
Eleven miles. We are going to be wrong about the weather.

INT. SNOW CAVE - NIGHT

The stove hisses. Dell does not answer.

MARA
You knew the cable was cut before we left.

INT. SNOW CAVE - LATER

Mara turns her back to the stove.

DELL
I knew somebody wanted the station quiet. That is not the same.

EXT. VALLEY FLOOR - DAY

A supply truck sits with both doors open, engine warm.

MARA
There is the truck. There is nobody in the truck.

INT. TRUCK - DAY

Dell reads a clipboard. The manifest is torn.

DELL
Keys in it. Half a tank. Somebody left in a hurry, on foot.

EXT. VALLEY FLOOR - DUSK

Mara takes the wheel. Dell does not get in.

MARA
We drive it back and we tell them the truth.

EXT. VALLEY FLOOR - CONTINUOUS

He steps back from the door.

DELL
The truth is the count. Nobody at the base wants the count.

INT. TRUCK - NIGHT

Headlights swing onto the road. Dell climbs in.

MARA
Then I will be the one who says it out loud.

INT. BASE RADIO ROOM - NIGHT

The room goes quiet. Somebody writes it down.

MARA
Eleven. The count was eleven, and we found one truck.
